NNAAMMEE
     uunnwwiinndd — validating DNS resolver

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     uunnwwiinndd [--ddnnvv] [--ff _f_i_l_e] [--ss _s_o_c_k_e_t]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     uunnwwiinndd is a validating DNS resolver.  It is intended to run on client ma‐
     chines like workstations or laptops and only listens on localhost.

     uunnwwiinndd sends DNS queries to nameservers to answer queries.  If it detects
     that DNS queries are blocked by the local network, it can switch to re‐
     solvers learned through autoconfiguration.  It periodically probes if DNS
     is no longer blocked and switches back to querying nameservers itself.  A
     list of sources for proposals learned through autoconfiguration is docu‐
     mented in resolvd(8).

     uunnwwiinndd keeps the DNS answers in a cache shared by the different DNS name
     server types.  uunnwwiinndd manages the cache size by deleting oldest entries
     when needed.  The cache is non-configurable and is lost upon process
     restart.

     To have uunnwwiinndd enabled at boot time, use “rcctl enable unwind”, which
     sets

           unwind_flags=""

     in rc.conf.local(8).

     A running uunnwwiinndd can be controlled with the unwindctl(8) utility.

     The options are as follows:

     --dd      Do not daemonize.  If this option is specified, uunnwwiinndd will run
             in the foreground and log to _s_t_d_e_r_r.

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Specify an alternative configuration file.

     --nn      Configtest mode.  Only check the configuration file for validity.

     --ss _s_o_c_k_e_t
             Use an alternate location for the default control socket.

     --vv      Produce more verbose output.  Multiple --vv options increase the
             verbosity.  Debug output from libunbound is only available when
             logging to _s_t_d_e_r_r.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/unwind.conf       Default uunnwwiinndd configuration file.
     /var/db/unwind.key     Trust anchor for DNSSEC validation.
     /dev/unwind.sock       UNIX-domain socket used for communication with
                            unwindctl(8).

